{
  "dom_id": "step-q74386",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{x+3}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14264217/assets/345fdaf3f301848b35448b710f240a308cfaadc1bae2729a5b0ddfa76793f6cb.png)\n\nSince we have $y=\\frac{1}{x+3},$ we need to translate the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ to the *left* by $3$ units.\n\nNow, since the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ is translated to the left by $3$ units, the vertical asymptote $x=0$ is also translated to the left by $3$ units. So, the equation of the new vertical asymptote is $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14264217/assets/93098cc2f4f5674c7b895d8340b0292c884bc1503b3a7677882b2392bc267446.png)",
